// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
// kernel/uprog.fi -- round 62: the programs that run in ring 3.
//
// This file is FIRN, and it is compiled into an OBJECT FILE OF ITS OWN.
// The linker script puts its code on pages of its own between
// `__user_begin` and `__user_end`, and the kernel sets the user bit for
// exactly those pages. That is the whole reason for the separate
// translation unit: a function that lay in the middle of the kernel's
// text could not be made reachable for ring 3 without handing the kernel
// over with it.
//
// Two rules follow from that, and both are checked in
// `tools/kernel/run.sh`:
//
//   1. NOTHING out of the kernel may be called here. No import, no
//      `serial.puts` -- the object file has to be free of undefined names
//      just like the kernel's, and everything it needs it does itself.
//   2. Everything the program says goes through a SYSTEM CALL. That is
//      the only door out of ring 3, and it is the door this round built.
//
// Round 59 had its user program in assembly, in `isr.s`, and the reason
// given was that a compiled function would sit in the middle of the
// kernel. That reason is gone -- what is left is a linker script with
// three lines more, and the user program is a language away from the
// kernel instead of an instruction set.

profile kernel

export { u_enter }

// The system call numbers, see sys.fi. 1 and 2 belong to the excursion of
// round 59 and stay what they were.
// ROUND K4: the numbers of Linux x86-64 (`kernel/sys.fi`). What
// round 62 called 11 is 1 now, and what it called 20 does not exist any
// more -- a directory is read with `getdents64` out of a descriptor, the
// way every Unix has read one since 1986.
